      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2011 The Authors Acta Obstetricia et Gynecologica Scandinavica\u2002© 2011 Nordic Federation of Societies of Obstetrics and Gynecology.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "Label": "UNLABELLED",
            "content": "OBJECTIVE. To evaluate plasma levels of lipocalin-2, which is a novel adipokine associated with obesity and insulin resistance, in pregnant women."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DESIGN",
            "content": "Prospective case-control study."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SETTING",
            "content": "University hospital."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "POPULATION",
            "content": "Pregnant women with pre-pregnancy body mass index >25kg/m(2) (overweight; n=29) and body mass index <25kg/m(2) (n=27), whose gestational ages were between 24 and 28 weeks, as study groups and nonpregnant control women with body mass index <25kg/m(2) (n=29)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Plasma lipocalin-2 levels, fasting plasma glucose and fasting plasma insulin levels; homeostasis model assessment insulin resistance index and fasting plasma glucose/fasting plasma insulin ratio were measured for each subject."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
            "content": "Comparisons among the groups and correlations for lipocalin-2 and the parameters of insulin resistance."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Plasma lipocalin-2 levels among the pregnant women were significantly higher than those of the control group (p<0.001 for both group comparisons). Lipocalin-2 levels were significantly higher in the group with pre-pregnancy body mass index >25kg/m(2) compared with the group with pre-pregnancy body mass index <25kg/m(2) (p=0.003). Lipocalin-2 levels were positively correlated with homeostasis model assessment insulin resistance index and fasting plasma insulin and negatively correlated with fasting plasma glucose/fasting plasma insulin ratio in both pregnant groups."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Lipocalin-2 was found to be higher in pregnant women, especially when pre-pregnancy body mass index was >25kg/m(2) , and it was correlated with markers of insulin resistance."
          }
        ]
      },
